// The navmesh package: finding the way. A program reaches it with 'import
// navmesh', and gets two things.
//
// 1. A navigation mesh of a 3-D world and the queries that use it, after
//    Recast and Detour (Mikko Mononen, recastnavigation, zlib licence), written
//    again in Cone from the published algorithms and Mononen's own notes:
//
//    - Input (input.cone): walkable triangles (a mesh, loose triangles, a
//      heightfield made into triangles), each with an area; obstacles, a
//      vertical circle or a box with a yaw between two heights, like the
//      browser's solids, that block or that mark an area (a door, mud, water);
//      the agent (radius, height, highest climb, steepest slope) and the
//      resolution (cell size and height, tile size).
//    - Generation, by tiles, each built alone from the input (the stages of
//      Recast): voxelise into columns of spans (voxel.cone), filter spans for
//      low obstacles, ledges and low ceilings (voxel.cone), join the open
//      spans, erode by the agent's radius, paint areas, partition into
//      regions (compact.cone, monotone partitioning), trace and simplify the
//      region outlines (contour.cone), triangulate by ear clipping and merge
//      into convex polygons of up to 6 vertices with their neighbours
//      (polymesh.cone), then place the tile in the mesh and link it to its
//      neighbours' portals (tile.cone, build.cone). One tile is rebuilt when
//      something in it changes: 'rebuildDirty'.
//    - Queries (query.cone, after Detour): the nearest polygon and the
//      closest point on one, A* over the polygons with a cost for each area
//      (an open or shut door area, mud, water are a query filter's
//      business, no rebuild needed), the funnel algorithm for the straight
//      path through the polygons it chose, a raycast along the mesh, moving
//      along the surface sliding on walls.
//    - Off-mesh connections (build.cone): a door, a ladder, a jump, joining
//      the polygons nearest its two ends, one way or two, switched on and off
//      while the program runs. A route through one has its two ends marked.
//    - Steering (steer.cone): a path corridor (Detour's) that follows an
//      agent along the mesh and shortcuts; the look-ahead point; arrival;
//      a Follower that walks a route; the walls near a point, for the
//      avoidance layer, which is not here.
//    - Looking (draw.cone): a top-down picture of a mesh and a path as a BMP,
//      and the mesh and path as line segments for a renderer's line drawing.
//
// 2. A grid pathfinder for tile worlds (grid.cone): A* with weights and
//    optional diagonals, and jump point search for the uniform case.
//
// How a door works: three ways, by what changes. A door that is solid geometry
// is an obstacle switched off and on ('NavInput.setObstacleEnabled' then
// 'rebuildDirty': only the tiles it touches rebuild, and references into them
// become stale, which 'NavMesh.isValid' and 'Corridor.isValid' tell). A door
// whose doorway has an area of its own (an obstacle that marks AreaDoor) is
// opened or shut in the query filter ('QueryFilter.setPassable'), with no
// rebuild. A door that is a link between two places is an off-mesh connection
// ('setOffMeshEnabled').
//
// Conventions are geomath's: right-handed, y up, metres, f32. Triangles are
// counterclockwise seen from above; polygons of the mesh are the same way.
// A flat floor's polygons are at the top of its voxels: heights are
// quantised to the cell height (0.2 m by default) and rounded up, and a
// polygon's surface is the one its vertices make (there is no detail mesh), so
// a corner's height can differ from the true ground by a voxel.
//
// A reference to a polygon is a 'u64' (NoRef is 0): its tile's salt, tile and
// number. A reference survives its tile's rebuild only as a stale one.
//
// Where it differs from Recast and Detour (all of it is listed in the design
// note, Design\navmesh.md): monotone partitioning only (no watershed, no
// layers); small islands dropped but small regions not merged into their
// neighbours; no holes in contours (monotone regions have none); no detail
// mesh; no BVH inside a tile (a query scans its tile's polygons); off-mesh
// connections are kept beside the tiles, not as polygons in them, and found by
// scanning; no crowd (that is the behave package's).
//
//   navmesh.cone   this overview
//   util.cone      small scalar helpers
//   input.cone     NavParams, Obstacle, NavInput
//   voxel.cone     the span heightfield, rasterising triangles and solids, the filters
//   compact.cone   open spans, erosion, areas, monotone regions
//   contour.cone   region outlines
//   polymesh.cone  ear clipping, merging, welding, adjacency, portals
//   tile.cone      Tile, NavMesh, building a tile, linking tiles
//   build.cone     building the whole mesh, rebuilding, off-mesh connections
//   query.cone     QueryFilter, nearest, A*, the funnel, raycast, moveAlongSurface
//   steer.cone     Corridor, look-ahead, arrival, Follower, local walls
//   draw.cone      Canvas, drawing a mesh and a path
//   grid.cone      the grid A* and jump point search
//   heap.cone      the binary heap

mod navmesh;

import libc;
import collections use *;
import geomath use *;
